01.a|ADI|Annotate every state transition in boot.S from GRUB entry to kernel_main, including retained magic/info, direction flag, GDT, CR3, EFER, CR0 and stack alignment. Give the first instruction invalidated by one missing prerequisite.|A bootloader entry is not an ISO C call; a correct page count cannot prove a legal mode transition.|2048 large-page entries cover four GiB; each control-register and far-transfer dependency has an explicit architectural source or unresolved authority.|Move the load address and derive every relocation, identity-map and reserved-range obligation.|Separate the entry-state ledger from the C ABI ledger.|The table arithmetic is necessary but does not establish that the CPU accepted the transition. Assembly, linker and runtime obligations must agree; unsupported architecture claims stay pending.
01.b|BFI|Build only the boot-smoke milestone using learner assembly and C. Retain linker script, header location/checksum, ELF/map, undefined symbols, disassembly and actual serial C-entry marker; compare GCC and Clang object dependencies.|A successful link or copied serial text does not establish boot execution.|The exact image named by the launch command reaches its own C marker; hosted startup and implicit unreviewed helpers are absent.|Change link section ordering and preserve a discoverable aligned Multiboot header.|Bind the serial observation to a hashed image and command.|Strict dependency closure includes assembly and compiler-generated calls. The early gate must not rely on a complete later kernel to conceal a missing bootstrap.
01.c|DFJ|In a disposable copy omit saving EBX, then separately omit the far jump. Predict the first divergence, capture register/serial evidence and repair one cause at a time; keep the good image as a control.|No output does not uniquely diagnose a page-table failure.|Each mutation is distinguished by supported machine state; repairing its cause restores the same smoke gate without relaxing expectations.|Diagnose a reviewer-selected stack alignment fault after seven days.|Find the last independently observed valid boundary.|A timeout is an observation, never success. A known mutation is not blind diagnosis; the withheld condition supplies the independent transfer opportunity.
02.a|BCF|Implement the tag walker with total-size and offset bounds checked before reading size or rounding. Test size7/8/21, truncated size fields, unknown tags and larger mmap stride; preserve a cursor ledger.|Rounding an unchecked size can wrap and can hide a raw out-of-bounds field read.|Offset8 plus size21 advances to32; malformed raw or rounded extents refuse before reading or publishing fields.|Use a buffer whose valid total size ends exactly on a tag boundary.|Check remaining bytes before arithmetic on untrusted sizes.|Unknown tags may be skipped only after their extents validate. Compatible extended records are not an excuse to skip minimum-stride checks.
02.b|BCF|Build a page reservation oracle from overlapping usable/reserved half-open physical intervals, plus kernel, module and boot-information spans. Permute input order and include partial-page overlaps.|Last-record-wins makes allocation depend on map order and can release live boot data.|Reserved overlap always wins; no partially reserved frame is returned; permutation preserves the final free set.|Move the module into a formerly free interval and recompute capacity.|Compare sets of page numbers, not only their count.|The reservation policy is a teaching allocator rule, not an inferred universal firmware ordering rule. Exact endpoint rounding determines ownership.
02.c|CFG|Snapshot parse outputs, insert a late duplicate required tag, missing terminator or overflowing base+length, and verify refusal preserves the documented output state and no allocation occurs.|Rejecting the final tag after publishing earlier records is not failure atomicity.|All malformed cases reject; the caller-visible result follows one explicit unchanged-or-cleared contract, with no mixed partial result.|Add a validated optional tag without making required-tag absence acceptable.|Stage the complete result before publication.|The contract must state output semantics on error. Bounds checking and transactionality are separate obligations.
03.a|ABF|Derive byte offsets of the 15 saved GPRs, vector/error pair and architectural frame from interrupts.S; compare a hardware-error and synthesized-zero entry, C offsetof values and 16-byte call alignment.|A function-call callee-save convention cannot describe an asynchronous interrupted context.|The declared frame is176 bytes and every offset matches the actual push/pop sequence; ISA and software fields are labeled separately.|Change diagnostic metadata by one word and repair call alignment without changing iret state.|Draw the stack in address order, not source push order.|A matching total size can conceal exchanged fields. The argument frame pointer must survive temporary alignment of the C-call stack.
03.b|BDF|Trigger one original user divide fault and one page fault in separate guests. Capture vector, error, RIP, CS and process identity through bounded serial output; verify return for a nonfatal interrupt and termination for the fault policy.|A diagnostic routine can corrupt the frame or block indefinitely while reporting it.|Saved state identifies each original trigger; no unbounded formatting dependency enters the freestanding image; normal interrupt return preserves all sentinels.|Exhaust the diagnostic output budget and preserve a minimal first-fault record.|Keep diagnosis independent of the failing allocation path.|Diagnostic completeness and bounded progress conflict; an explicit truncation policy is preferable to an unbounded dependency inside the exception path.
03.c|ADG|Compare the same exception class from user and kernel mode in disposable images. Show user termination and peer progress, but a fatal kernel diagnostic stop; retain first-fault state if a second fault occurs during reporting.|Continuing after an unknown kernel invariant failure is not equivalent to containing an untrusted process.|Privilege origin and policy, not merely vector number, select containment; the double-fault path has independent stack ownership.|Inject a fault while the ordinary kernel stack is unavailable and state which observation remains possible.|Start with saved CS privilege bits and the selected IST entry.|The original single-core diagnostic kernel is not a general recoverable kernel. A known user fault may be contained while the integrated self-test still flags an unexpected event.
04.a|BCF|Implement reserved/free/allocated frame states; exhaust [3MiB,4MiB), release in a permuted order, and reject unaligned, reserved, foreign and duplicate releases with unchanged state.|An aligned integer is not proof of frame ownership.|Exactly256 distinct frames emerge initially; conservation and exclusive membership hold after every operation.|Introduce a reserved hole and derive the exact returned set, not just a smaller total.|Use a separate bitset oracle for ownership.|Allocator correctness depends on the reservation map and legal transitions. Zero as failure must not accidentally reserve a valid frame under a changed address policy.
04.b|BCG|Inject frame exhaustion at each mapping allocation for a multi-page user request. Snapshot PTEs/free frames, enforce zero-fill before exposure, and require rollback of newly acquired pages. Distinguish boot-loader panic policy from a recoverable syscall allocation API.|A finite host allocator pass does not establish transactional guest mapping.|Each rejected request leaves prior mappings and free count intact; success exposes only zeroed pages with intended permissions.|Allocate a range crossing an existing mapping and refuse without damaging it.|Record acquired frames in a bounded rollback ledger.|The retained loader may choose fatal admission failure; that does not satisfy a newly claimed recoverable operation. Separate those policies explicitly.
04.c|AGJ|Trace process exit through marking dead, selecting a peer, changing CR3/TSS stack and reclaiming frames. Identify static page-table/kernel-stack storage versus allocated user frames and show which objects the current code actually reclaims.|A generic claim that all process storage is freed can hide static retention or premature reuse.|No current address-space or execution-stack storage is reused; accounting names intentionally static retained storage.|Design dynamic process admission and derive the extra teardown and slot-generation obligations.|Classify each resource by allocation origin before writing cleanup.|Reclamation requires quiescence of all users. Single-core scheduling establishes only the stated local handoff; SMP TLB/users require later mechanisms.
05.a|ABEF|Derive effective permissions along all page-table levels for the process window, kernel identity region, text, data and guard. In an intentionally flawed guest map a dedicated synthetic kernel sentinel page as user-writable; compare corrected permissions.|A user bit at an upper level alone neither grants every leaf nor proves safe supervisor mappings.|The flawed isolated image permits only the declared sentinel violation; the corrected image rejects it while normal user data remains writable.|Move the sentinel behind a different upper-level table and repeat the full effective-permission argument.|Effective access depends on every traversed level and the selected control-register state.|The demonstration proves a permission-policy violation in original teaching code, not exploitation of a third-party kernel. Keep the sentinel away from executable or control data.
05.b|BEF|Run two original workers storing different values at the same virtual address and a third attempting the synthetic supervisor write. Record CR3 identities, saved fault address/code, sentinel contents and peer counters in flawed/corrected guest copies.|A crash alone does not establish a successful isolation violation or successful containment.|Corrected supervisor write reports the expected present/write/user fault code7; peers retain distinct data and progress. The flawed control demonstrates the sentinel change directly.|Attempt data-page instruction fetch and derive its different fault evidence.|Pair the negative target with a positive permitted access.|Changing a harmless sentinel is sufficient evidence of the declared property failure. Do not infer arbitrary code execution or host escape from it.
05.c|ACEI|Analyze a stack jump that skips a one-page guard and lands in another mapped region. Compare with a sequential overflow reaching the guard; state exact writable regions and independent compiler/runtime checks.|One guard page is not a proof that every out-of-range stack access is detected.|The guard detects accesses to its unmapped span only; the skipped-guard counterexample invalidates universal memory-safety claims.|Add explicit stack probing and identify the size assumptions needed for its argument.|Protection applies to touched addresses, not the abstract intent of an allocation.|Guard pages are one mitigation under a layout and access model. C object bounds and lifetime remain separate obligations inside mapped pages.
06.a|ABF|Separate a pure next-runnable policy from saved-frame/address-space transfer. Replay alive=[true,false,true],current0 for four selections and then exercise zero/one runnable states.|A correct round-robin sequence cannot establish preserved registers or a valid CR3.|Sequence2,0,2,0 follows the model; no-runnable policy is explicit; machine transfer is separately observed.|Replace round-robin with weighted choice while preserving the same context mechanism.|Use independent policy and mechanism ledgers.|The scheduler chooses an identity; assembly and mapping code preserve its execution state. Their tests have different failure surfaces.
06.b|BDF|Run nonyielding users with GPR and private-memory sentinels; record timer-origin switches and PIC acknowledgements. Mutate one saved register or omit EOI in separate disposable builds.|Frequent syscalls or cooperative yield can falsely look like preemption.|Both workers advance under actual timer interrupts; each injected defect has a distinct trace and repaired control.|Add an exited slot between runnable peers and retain correct stack ownership.|Tag switch causes before comparing worker counters.|Timer delivery, interrupt acknowledgement, save/restore and policy must all work. Progress observations do not prove bounded fairness over all executions.
06.c|AHI|Measure a peer's delay during a bounded synchronous kernel poll and compare with an equal-length user loop. Report clock source, emulator scheduling, poll count and nonpreemptible interval separately.|A polling iteration bound is neither wall-clock WCET nor a real-time scheduling guarantee.|The nonpreemptible kernel interval can postpone user scheduling; no physical bound is inferred from guest elapsed times.|Change quantum and device completion delay independently and explain which latency component changes.|Keep service time and scheduling delay in separate columns.|The design intentionally trades simplicity for latency. An analytical bound requires limits on device interaction, interrupts and execution costs beyond a finite QEMU run.
07.a|BCF|Construct ELF64 byte fixtures that fail late: ninth PT_LOAD, filesz>memsz, wrapped offsets, entry outside executable bytes, overlapping pages and a writable executable segment. Validate all headers before mapping and snapshot frame state on refusal.|Validating each segment while immediately mapping can leave partial state after a later invalid segment.|Each invalid image refuses without committed mappings; all accepted extents and entry permissions satisfy the narrower CMK profile.|Create nonoverlapping byte extents that share a page and explain the permission conflict.|Compare rounded page spans as well as byte spans.|General ELF allows cases the educational loader intentionally rejects. Validation must enforce the selected profile before allocation or explicitly roll back.
07.b|BCF|Implement CMKA1 lookup with unique bounded names and disjoint table/payload spans. Load filesz1,memsz4096 and verify the remaining4095 bytes are zero; test a zero-fill tail spanning two pages.|Section names and disk bytes alone do not define the loaded memory image.|The archive fixture starts payload at56 with total64; lookup rejects duplicates/overlap and the mapped tail is zero-filled.|Use an accepted ELF without section headers and show program-header loading still works.|Separate archive extent validation from ELF extent validation.|The container yields a borrowed checked byte span. The loader owns new zeroed frames; it must not serialize native structures or depend on .bss section names.
07.c|AIJ|Compare the exact CMK acceptance table with the inspected generic ELF program-header source: ET_EXEC, TLS, interpreter, alignment and permissions. Provide a valid general ELF case intentionally refused by CMK.|Supporting one ELF profile is not general executable or filesystem support.|Every rejected general feature has a deliberate bounded reason; generic ABI allowed permissions are distinguished from the stricter W^X policy.|Design a read-only additional archive entry type without implying dynamic linking support.|Use a feature matrix with authority and implementation columns.|The generic ABI is broader and permits platform-specific choices. The project must identify its subset honestly rather than silently accepting unsupported metadata.
08.a|ABEF|Specify int0x80 register/error ABI and checked copy-in/out. Use spans ending exactly at a page edge, crossing into a guard, wrapping length and targeting read-only text; stage validation before any observable output.|Checking only the first byte or pointer range misses later-page permissions and partial side effects.|Corrected code rejects invalid spans before output mutation; an isolated flawed first-page-only helper exposes only a synthetic canary change in the bounded teaching model.|Change zero-length span policy and propagate it consistently through all services.|Validate arithmetic, then every covered page, then perform the operation.|The model counterexample must never dereference an arbitrary host pointer. Guest addresses belong to the disposable custom OS; copy semantics are not ISO C guarantees.
08.b|BCEF|Implement process-local slot/generation handles and close/reuse tests. Derive token65537 for slot0 generation1, reject high-bit truncation, foreign process tokens and old tokens after reuse; specify generation exhaustion.|A generation counter with wraparound eventually allows stale-token aliasing.|All invalid tokens leave service state unchanged; exhausted generations retire or refuse rather than silently wrapping into validity.|Allow a delegated read-only handle and derive explicit rights propagation.|Token validity includes owner, rights, slot, liveness and generation.|The integer encoding is only one component of authorization. A valid slot number cannot bypass process-local ownership.
08.c|BEFG|In original user programs issue unknown calls, kernel pointers, oversized allocation and closed handles while a peer updates a synthetic counter. Snapshot kernel resource counts and sentinel data before/after each rejection.|An error return alone does not prove absence of side effects or peer corruption.|Corrected image preserves sentinel/resource invariants and peer progress; any flawed control demonstrates only its declared local property violation.|Combine a valid handle with an invalid destination and verify neither authority nor state is consumed.|Compare full before/after state, not only RAX.|Validation order matters when an operation can consume resources. The laboratory has no host devices, credentials or external targets; no privilege-escalation claim follows from mere failure.
09.a|BCF|Build synthetic PCI capability chains with loops, truncated BAR windows, duplicate required capabilities, absent VERSION_1/FLUSH and overflowing notification multiplication. Derive an initialization state machine and reject before DRIVER_OK.|Finding a virtio identifier is not sufficient capability, feature or extent validation.|Traversal is bounded, unsupported cases remain nonoperational, and negotiated feature/status trace corresponds to the selected modern PCI profile.|Change a BAR base while preserving legal extents and recompute notification address.|Treat every capability as a checked span before interpreting its fields.|Transport discovery, feature negotiation and queue activation are distinct contracts. New physical devices require their own exact specification and errata review.
09.b|BCFG|Trace ownership of header, bounce page, status, descriptors and ring slots across one request; exercise16-bit index65535→0 with eight slots7→0. Inject a duplicate/wrong used ID and a late completion after timeout.|Ring-slot wrap does not permit reusing still-owned DMA storage.|Publication precedes availability; exactly the outstanding identity can complete; timeout retains storage and poisons further submission.|Permit two outstanding requests and derive per-request storage/generation needs before implementing it.|Write an owner for every buffer at every transition.|One outstanding request simplifies the proof but does not eliminate late-device writes. Timeout alone supplies no quiescence certificate.
09.c|AFI|Annotate each descriptor store, compiler barrier, CPU ordering instruction, MMIO notification and completion read in the actual driver. Explain the x86 coherent identity-DMA assumptions and construct a reordered abstract countertrace if publication ordering is removed.|volatile alone is not a cross-device happens-before or cache-maintenance protocol.|The required order has separate compiler, CPU and device arguments; the abstract failing trace is distinguished from an observed QEMU failure.|Port the argument to a noncoherent architecture and list cache/IOMMU changes requiring new evidence.|Separate C visibility from bus-visible ordering.|An unchanged C loop does not establish portability. The selected mfence and identity address relation are target choices, not ISO C semantics.
10.a|ACG|Draw request states submitted, completed-success, completed-error, timed-out-unknown and flush-confirmed. Inject timeout after publication; prohibit buffer reuse and show why an error is not proof that no write occurred.|Request failure, device quiescence and persistent absence are distinct facts.|Late completion cannot corrupt recycled memory because storage stays reserved; durable claims require the selected flush boundary.|Add a reset acknowledgement contract and identify exactly when reclamation becomes justified.|Unknown state must preserve ownership until an independent terminal proof.|The retained poison policy sacrifices availability to retain ownership. A new retry policy needs operation identity and recovery semantics.
10.b|BCF|Implement checked page-to-sector conversion and the67-page slice starting at16. Test page1023/1024 of a4MiB image, slice endpoints15/16/82/83 and large caller integers before narrowing.|A valid page index after truncation can conceal an invalid original argument.|The image has1024 pages, eight sectors/page, and exclusive slice end83; out-of-range arguments cannot wrap into it.|Change sector size in a model and refuse unsupported real-device geometry.|Check capacity in wide units before multiplication and conversion.|The adapter must not let VM/store clients choose arbitrary device pages. Units and authority boundaries compose at the same call.
10.c|FGI|Run patterned write→flush→read and a fresh-guest reboot with the identical ordinary image. Retain backend cache options and full byte comparison; remove flush in a control and explain why a surviving value is still insufficient.|Persistence after one reboot does not prove durability under power loss or reordered writes.|The receipt proves only the executed QEMU/backend sequence; a no-flush lucky survival cannot satisfy the contract.|Use the storage simulator to cut a declared write/flush event and compare its distinct failure model.|Record which volatile state the reboot actually discards.|Guest restart, host process death and physical power failure are different experiments. The source and image identities are part of the observation.
11.a|BCF|Implement the64-byte shell buffer and explicit overflow/reset policy using only the CMK ABI. Specify whether capacity includes terminator; test empty line, maximum accepted line, overlong line followed by help and partial input across reads.|A bounded array alone does not define safe framing or recovery after overflow.|No command reads outside initialized bytes; the chosen discard/reset policy prevents an overlong tail from becoming an unintended command.|Accept CRLF while retaining a precise split-chunk policy.|Write the parser state after every input byte.|The shell owns transient command storage. Any asynchronous command extension would need a different borrow/ownership contract.
11.b|BFG|Drive help, echo and exit through the actual serial input channel. Assert archive names, parser responses, process handle revocation and post-switch frame accounting; distinguish unavailable input from EOF/exit.|A scripted expected output file is not an interactive guest test.|Each response is causally tied to injected input; exited process resources follow the documented deferred-reap policy.|Interrupt a partial line with process termination and verify cleanup.|Retain both sent bytes and raw received bytes with image identity.|A shell response tests a composed path, while frame accounting detects lifecycle defects that a text-only assertion misses.
11.c|AJ|Design one bounded read-only statistics command returning copied numeric data. State its ABI, buffer limit, failure semantics and privileges; compare boot archive lookup with dynamic process creation and a general filesystem.|Adding a command does not create fork/exec, pathname resolution or general file ownership.|The extension exposes no raw kernel pointer and does not overclaim services absent from the kernel.|Add a versioned result structure and reject unsupported versions without partial output.|Identify which component owns each reported fact.|An interface can preserve information hiding even in a small OS. Representation exposure creates new lifetime and authority coupling.
12.a|BFJ|Link the same hashed CVM core into a ring3 ELF through explicit adapters; run identical verified bytecode hosted and in guest and compare semantic result/error traces.|A separately rewritten interpreter is not evidence of portability of the original component.|Only adapter and target build choices differ; the result relation and bytecode identity match.|Load bytecode from a checked archive span and reject a truncated image before running.|Track source identity as well as output identity.|Portable semantics require explicit integer, storage and callback assumptions. Equivalent output on one input is insufficient to establish identical implementations.
12.b|AHI|Audit undefined symbols, map sections, static workspace and worst permitted nested call paths for CVM plus user adapter. Include verifier scratch, exception/kernel stacks separately and inspect generated helper calls.|A large user stack or successful link is not a resource proof.|Every linked helper has an owned allowed implementation; separate stack and static budgets fit the selected process layout under stated bounds.|Tighten the user stack and move suitable scratch to caller-owned static storage with a nonreentrancy contract.|Sum simultaneously live storage, not all functions independently.|Compiler stack reports need call-graph and recursion assumptions. Moving storage changes reentrancy and ownership even when outputs remain equal.
12.c|BCFG|Execute callback increment41→42 with allowance1, then zero allowance and callback failure. Count actual callbacks and ensure failed calls do not masquerade as success; separate instruction fuel from host allowance.|A host-call count cannot bound arbitrary callback execution time.|The successful case consumes one allowance; zero allowance yields CVM_HOST_BUDGET with no callback; error propagates distinctly.|Use a callback that consumes a bounded internal loop and derive a combined service bound.|Record dispatch, budget charge and callback side effect in order.|Resource counters bound only their defined units. A blocking or unbounded callback invalidates a wall-time claim even with one permitted call.
13.a|ABJ|Draw the full bytecode→named service→store→page adapter→syscall→virtio path, with ownership and error translation at every edge. Verify the adapter confines pages to[16,83) and uses caller-owned workspace.|Composition can bypass a lower-layer authority or silently change failure guarantees.|VM operands never become raw kernel pointers/device pages; every layer preserves its stated ownership and error contract.|Add a read-only service client while keeping exclusive transaction ownership.|List both normal and failed return meanings at each edge.|Reused components retain their original objective credit; new assessment concerns interface interactions and resource coexistence.
13.b|BFG|Run the two-boot commit/lost-reply experiment with shared VM/store sources and same disk. Preserve first CVM_HOST_ERROR, actual successful store commit/flush evidence, second VM GET42 and store-check output.|A lost callback reply is not a simulated failure of an acknowledged disk flush.|The executed trace supports committed42 despite first-call error; raw source/image/syscall/driver identities are retained.|Cut before commit acknowledgement in the storage simulator and compare allowed old/new outcomes.|Separate the internal commit fact from the caller's knowledge.|The intentional injected reply failure is a composition test. Its known state is stronger than what an arbitrary caller could infer from HOST_ERROR alone.
13.c|ACJ|Give caller histories with identical HOST_ERROR but one failed-before-commit and one committed-before-reply-loss. Design a query or durable request-ID protocol before retrying a non-idempotent increment.|Retry after uncertainty can duplicate an effect even if each transaction is atomic.|The two histories are observationally indistinguishable to the original caller; blind retry cannot promise exactly-once effects.|Persist a deduplication record atomically with the value and analyze retention/exhaustion.|Atomic commit and delivery acknowledgement answer different questions.|A request-ID scheme needs scope, uniqueness and recovery rules. Renaming an error as retryable does not supply those mechanisms.
14.a|DFJ|Diagnose a withheld original kernel mutation using host contract tests, guest saved state and a minimal causal trace. Explain an alternative hypothesis and the observation that rules it out.|A host parser pass cannot localize an assembly or device defect.|The report identifies the first violated boundary and a repair that survives both relevant host and guest controls.|After seven days change a resource limit and repeat without opening the assessor implementation.|Choose an observation that separates hypotheses before adding logging.|Debugging competence is assessed by causal discrimination and transfer, not only by restoring a known marker.
14.b|DFI|Build from a fresh directory, bind source/image/tool hashes and deliberately test a stub, malformed ELF and stale handle. Reject timeout, stale logs and mismatched image receipts.|A previous green receipt is not evidence for changed sources.|Every positive result names its current image; each meaningful negative control fails for its expected reason.|Change compiler optimization and explain any changed observation point.|Make provenance a checked input to interpretation.|Negative controls must reach the mechanism they claim to test. Build failure or an unrelated crash cannot stand in for a semantic rejection.
14.c|AFJ|Prepare a delayed assessor-held variant combining one mapping-limit change and one known interface contract. Require prediction, implementation, failure injection and an explicit list of physical/SMP/general-OS claims still unassessed.|Finite passing controls do not establish universal kernel correctness or learner mastery.|The objective-specific rubric distinguishes reproduction from independent changed-condition reasoning; unadministered assessment remains pending.|Integrate a later T56 process/thread mechanism and revisit every single-core assumption used here.|Write which old proof premise changed before reusing its conclusion.|The original route remains a bounded prerequisite. Later extensions add evidence rather than retroactively broadening historical receipts.
